\chapter{Project Selection Phase}

\section{Software Bid}

\subsection*{Higher-Order Goal}

Airport operations involve multiple interconnected activities including flight management, passenger luggage handling, visitor management, staff management, and ground transportation. When these activities are handled through disconnected systems or manual records, airport personnel may face difficulties in maintaining consistent and accessible operational information.

\textbf{Smart Airport Management System (SAMS)} is developed as a centralized web-based management system for organizing important airport operational information through a single application. The system provides authenticated users with role-based access to modules for flights, luggage, visitors, staff, and transportation.

The primary goal of SAMS is to provide a structured platform through which airport-related records can be created, viewed, updated, and deleted according to the responsibilities of different users.

The project is therefore not limited to the development of individual CRUD pages. Its main objective is to demonstrate how different airport-management activities can be brought together under a common software architecture. The system establishes a clear relationship between the user interface, application programming interface, authorization layer, and persistent database.

From a software engineering perspective, the project also demonstrates the application of modular design principles. Rather than implementing all functionality in a single component, the system separates routes, controllers, middleware, database models, frontend pages, and authentication logic. This separation makes the prototype easier to understand and provides a foundation for future extensions.

\subsection*{Time-to-Value}

The development of SAMS follows a modular implementation strategy:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{Authentication first:} User registration, login, JWT-based authentication, and protected routes establish the security foundation of the system.
    \item \textbf{Core operational modules second:} Flight, luggage, visitor, staff, and transportation modules provide the main airport-management functionality.
    \item \textbf{Role-based authorization:} Different operations are restricted according to user roles such as administrator, staff, airline staff, passenger, visitor, and transportation operator.
    \item \textbf{Frontend integration:} A React-based interface communicates with the Express backend through REST APIs.
    \item \textbf{Dashboard and reporting:} The frontend provides a centralized dashboard and dedicated management pages for viewing operational information.
\end{itemize}

This order of implementation reduces dependency between development tasks. Authentication establishes the identity of the user before protected resources are introduced. Once the common authentication mechanism is available, the operational modules can use the same middleware rather than implementing separate security mechanisms.

The modular approach also makes it possible to verify the system incrementally. For example, the authentication routes can be tested independently before flight or luggage operations are introduced. Similarly, each resource module has its own route, controller, and model, allowing errors to be isolated more easily.

\subsection*{Problem Statement}

Airport management requires the coordination of multiple categories of operational data. Without a centralized system, information related to flights, luggage, visitors, staff, and transportation may become difficult to maintain and access.

The major problems addressed by SAMS are:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Airport operational records may be distributed across separate systems or manual records.
    \item Different categories of users require different levels of access to airport information.
    \item Flight and transportation information needs to be associated with relevant operational records.
    \item Luggage records require tracking through identifiable luggage tags and status information.
    \item Visitor and staff records require structured storage and controlled modification.
    \item A centralized dashboard can provide users with a clearer overview of airport operations.
\end{itemize}

These problems have a common software requirement: operational information should be stored in a structured form and accessed through controlled application interfaces. A centralized system can also reduce duplication in data-entry processes and make it easier to retrieve information when required.

Another important consideration is that not every user should have identical capabilities. An administrator may need to perform operations that are not appropriate for a passenger or visitor. Therefore, access control is treated as a backend responsibility in SAMS rather than only a visual restriction in the frontend.

\subsection*{Proposed Solution}

SAMS provides a centralized web application consisting of a React frontend, an Express.js backend, and a MongoDB database accessed through Mongoose.

The system uses REST APIs to communicate between the frontend and backend. Authentication is implemented using JSON Web Tokens (JWT), while password hashing is handled using \texttt{bcryptjs}. Role-based middleware restricts access to protected operations.

The major modules implemented in the system are:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Authentication and user management
    \item Flight management
    \item Luggage management
    \item Visitor management
    \item Staff management
    \item Transportation management
    \item Dashboard and reports
    \item Application settings
\end{itemize}

The architecture deliberately keeps the database behind the backend API. The React application does not directly connect to MongoDB. Instead, an API request travels from the frontend to an Express route, passes through the applicable middleware, reaches the controller, and is then processed using a Mongoose model.

This approach provides a clear boundary between the presentation layer and data layer. It also makes the authorization mechanism meaningful because access to the actual database operations occurs only through the backend.

