\subsection*{Evaluation Criteria}

The prototype is evaluated primarily on the correctness of its core management workflows:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Successful user registration and login.
    \item Correct JWT-based authentication.
    \item Correct role-based access to protected operations.
    \item Successful creation, retrieval, updating, and deletion of operational records.
    \item Correct communication between the React frontend and Express REST API.
    \item Correct persistence of records in MongoDB.
    \item Usability and consistency of the management dashboard.
\end{itemize}

The evaluation therefore considers both functional correctness and architectural correctness. A page being visible in the frontend is not by itself sufficient; the corresponding backend operation must also enforce the intended access restrictions.

\subsection*{Scalability}

The system follows a modular architecture in which frontend pages communicate with backend API routes rather than directly accessing the database. This separation allows individual modules to be modified without requiring the complete application to be redesigned.

The REST API architecture also allows additional airport-management modules to be introduced in later versions.

The current architecture is particularly suitable for incremental development because each major resource follows a recognizable pattern. A new resource can generally be represented using a model, controller, route group, frontend API operation, and frontend page.

Scalability in the current report refers primarily to architectural extensibility rather than demonstrated large-scale production capacity. No claim is made that the prototype has been load-tested for a production airport environment.

\subsection*{Risks and Mitigations}

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{Unauthorized access:} Protected API routes use JWT authentication and role-based middleware.
    \item \textbf{Invalid user input:} Mongoose schemas define required fields, data types, enumerations, and constraints for stored records.
    \item \textbf{Duplicate records:} Unique constraints are used for fields such as flight numbers, luggage tags, employee IDs, vehicle numbers, and visitor pass IDs.
    \item \textbf{Frontend-backend mismatch:} API communication is centralized through the Axios API layer to maintain consistent request handling.
    \item \textbf{Database unavailability:} The backend reports database connection errors rather than starting as if the database were available.
\end{itemize}

Risk identification is important because the system contains several dependent layers. A failure in the database layer can affect the backend, which in turn affects the frontend. Similarly, an incorrect authentication token can prevent otherwise valid frontend requests from reaching their intended controllers.

The use of schemas, middleware, and centralized API communication reduces some of these risks by placing validation and access control in predictable locations.

\subsection*{Project Development Perspective}

From a software engineering perspective, SAMS was developed as an integration-oriented project. The final application is the result of connecting several smaller components rather than building one monolithic feature.

The major development activities can be viewed as a sequence:

\[
\text{Requirement Analysis}
\rightarrow
\text{System Design}
\rightarrow
\text{Backend Development}
\rightarrow
\text{Frontend Development}
\rightarrow
\text{Integration}
\rightarrow
\text{Testing}
\]

Each stage depends on the previous stage. For example, the frontend cannot reliably communicate with a backend endpoint unless the endpoint structure and expected data format are understood. Similarly, role-based frontend navigation is meaningful only when corresponding backend authorization exists.

\subsection*{Summary}

SAMS provides a centralized airport-management platform that combines authentication, role-based authorization, operational CRUD modules, database persistence, and a web-based dashboard. The current prototype establishes the core software architecture required for further airport-management functionality.

The project selection is therefore justified by the need to demonstrate a realistic multi-module information-management application. It combines database design, backend development, frontend development, authentication, authorization, API integration, and software testing within one coherent system.


\section{Project Overview}

SAMS is organized around several core airport entities: flights, luggage, visitors, staff, transportation, and users.

The system is designed around the idea that these entities are operationally related. Flights form one of the central entities because luggage and transportation records can contain references to flights. Staff and visitor records represent other operational categories, while User records support application-level authentication and authorization.

\subsection*{Who Uses It}

The system supports multiple user roles:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{Administrator:} Has the highest level of access and can manage major airport records.
    \item \textbf{Staff:} Can perform operational management activities allowed by the system.
    \item \textbf{Airline Staff:} Can access flight-related operations according to authorization rules.
    \item \textbf{Passenger:} Has access to passenger-relevant information and protected resources.
    \item \textbf{Visitor:} Has restricted access to visitor-relevant resources.
    \item \textbf{Transportation Operator:} Can manage transportation-related information according to role permissions.
\end{itemize}

The role names correspond to the roles defined in the User model. The role is included in the authenticated user information and is subsequently used by the authorization middleware.

This distinction between user identity and resource data is important. A flight record, for example, describes an operational object, whereas a User record describes an account that is allowed to access the application. The two concepts are therefore represented separately in the database design.

\subsection*{How It Actually Works, End to End}

A typical session begins when a user registers or logs into the system. During login, the backend validates the credentials and generates a JWT token. The frontend stores the authenticated session information and attaches the token to subsequent API requests.

When a user accesses a protected module, the backend authentication middleware verifies the JWT. The role middleware then determines whether the authenticated user has permission to perform the requested operation.

The frontend communicates with the backend through REST APIs. The backend controllers process requests and interact with MongoDB through Mongoose models.

For example, the flight module provides APIs for creating, viewing, updating, and deleting flight records. Similar CRUD operations are available for luggage, visitors, staff, and transportation, subject to role restrictions.

The dashboard combines information obtained from the backend modules and presents the information through a centralized user interface.

The complete request path can therefore be summarized as:

\[
\text{User}
\rightarrow
\text{React UI}
\rightarrow
\text{Axios}
\rightarrow
\text{Express Route}
\rightarrow
\text{Middleware}
\rightarrow
\text{Controller}
\rightarrow
\text{Mongoose}
\rightarrow
\text{MongoDB}
\]

The response follows the reverse direction until it reaches the frontend, where it is presented to the user.

\subsection*{System Components}

The system can be divided into the following components:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item React frontend
    \item Axios API communication layer
    \item Authentication context and route guards
    \item Express REST API
    \item Authentication and role middleware
    \item Controllers
    \item Mongoose models
    \item MongoDB database
\end{enumerate}

These components are not isolated. They form a layered system in which each component has a specific responsibility.

The frontend is responsible for presentation and user interaction. The API client handles communication. The Express server provides the API boundary. Middleware performs common request checks. Controllers contain resource-level processing, while Mongoose models represent the database structure.

This division helps prevent application logic from becoming concentrated in one file or layer.


