\subsection*{Role-Based Interaction Analysis}

The role model is one of the most important parts of SAMS because the application supports several categories of users.

The administrator has the broadest management responsibility. Staff users can perform operational tasks permitted by the backend. Airline staff are associated primarily with flight-related access, while passengers and visitors receive more restricted access. Transportation operators have access to transportation-related functionality.

The exact permissions are implemented at the backend route level. This is important because frontend visibility alone is not considered sufficient security. Even if a button or page were hidden from a user, the backend must still reject an unauthorized request.

\section{Activity Diagram and Swimlane Diagram}

Figure~\ref{fig:activity} illustrates the general workflow of SAMS from authentication through access to an airport-management module.

The activity begins with user authentication. After successful login, the system identifies the user's role and permits access to the modules allowed for that role. The user can then perform operations such as viewing or modifying flight, luggage, visitor, staff, or transportation records. Requests are sent through the frontend API layer to the backend, where authentication, authorization, validation, and database operations are performed.

The swimlane representation separates responsibilities between the user, frontend, backend, and database. This is useful because the same user action may require multiple technical steps.

For example, when a user submits a new flight form, the visible action takes place in the React interface. The actual persistence, however, takes place through an HTTP request to the Express API and a database operation through Mongoose.

