\section{Software Requirement Specification (IEEE 830 Format)}

\subsection{Introduction}

\textbf{Purpose.} This section specifies the functional and non-functional requirements of the Smart Airport Management System.

\textbf{Document Conventions.} ``Shall'' denotes a mandatory requirement. Requirements are identified using FR for functional requirements and NFR for non-functional requirements.

\textbf{Intended Audience.} The report is intended for project evaluators, developers, testers, and future maintainers of the system.

\textbf{Product Scope.} The current prototype covers authentication, role-based authorization, flight management, luggage management, visitor management, staff management, transportation management, dashboard functionality, reports, and application settings.

\subsection{Overall Description}

\textbf{Product Perspective.} SAMS is a web application composed of a React frontend, an Express.js REST API backend, and a MongoDB database.

\textbf{User Classes and Characteristics.} The system supports administrator, staff, airline staff, passenger, visitor, and transportation operator roles. Each role receives access according to the authorization rules defined by the backend.

\textbf{Operating Environment.} The frontend operates in a modern web browser. The backend runs on Node.js and communicates with MongoDB.

\textbf{Design and Implementation Constraints.} The system depends on network communication between frontend and backend and on the availability of the configured MongoDB database.

\textbf{Assumptions and Dependencies.} Users are expected to have valid credentials and sufficient permissions for protected operations. The backend requires a configured database connection and JWT secret.

\subsection*{Functional Scope by Module}

The functional scope can be viewed as a set of related management responsibilities rather than independent pages.

Authentication establishes the user identity required for protected operations. Flight management provides the central operational flight records. Luggage management connects passenger luggage information with flights. Visitor and staff management maintain personnel-related operational records. Transportation management records vehicles and their associated operational information.

Dashboard and reports then provide a presentation layer over the information available from these modules.

\subsection*{Data Requirements}

The application requires structured information for both authentication and airport operations.

For authentication, the primary information consists of name, email, password, role, and timestamps. For operational resources, each model has its own required fields and selected enumerated values.

The database design uses unique identifiers where duplicate values would be problematic. Examples include flight numbers, luggage tags, employee IDs, vehicle numbers, and visitor pass IDs.

The use of enumerated status fields also reduces inconsistency. Instead of allowing arbitrary status strings, models restrict status values to predefined alternatives.

\subsection{External Interface Requirements}

\textbf{User Interfaces.} The application provides authentication pages, dashboard, flight management, luggage management, visitor management, staff management, transportation management, reports, and settings interfaces.

\textbf{Software Interfaces.} The React frontend communicates with the Express REST API using Axios. The backend communicates with MongoDB through Mongoose.

\textbf{Communication Interfaces.} Frontend-backend communication uses HTTP requests with JSON request and response data.

The API boundary allows the frontend to remain independent of database implementation details. A frontend component does not need to know how MongoDB stores the record; it only needs to communicate with the appropriate API endpoint and interpret the returned response.

