\section{System Architecture}

The SAMS architecture follows a three-layer web application model.

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{Presentation Layer:} React frontend responsible for user interaction, routing, forms, dashboard views, and API communication.
    \item \textbf{Application Layer:} Node.js and Express.js backend responsible for routing, authentication, authorization, controllers, validation, and business operations.
    \item \textbf{Data Layer:} MongoDB accessed through Mongoose models for persistent storage.
\end{enumerate}

The frontend does not communicate directly with MongoDB. Requests pass through the backend REST API, allowing authentication and authorization rules to be enforced centrally.

The layered architecture also improves separation of concerns. The presentation layer does not need to implement database logic, while the database layer does not need to know how information is displayed.

\subsection*{Request Processing Model}

A protected request follows the following conceptual sequence:

\[
\text{Frontend Request}
\rightarrow
\text{Express Route}
\rightarrow
\text{JWT Authentication}
\rightarrow
\text{Role Authorization}
\rightarrow
\text{Controller}
\rightarrow
\text{Mongoose Model}
\rightarrow
\text{MongoDB}
\]

The response then travels back through the controller and Express server to the frontend.

This model is repeated across the major CRUD modules. The main difference between modules is the resource being processed and the roles allowed to perform each operation.

\subsection*{Backend Architecture}

The backend is organized into:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item \texttt{config/} -- database configuration.
    \item \texttt{controllers/} -- request processing and CRUD operations.
    \item \texttt{middleware/} -- authentication and role authorization.
    \item \texttt{models/} -- Mongoose database schemas.
    \item \texttt{routes/} -- REST API endpoints.
    \item \texttt{server.js} -- Express server initialization and route registration.
\end{itemize}

The backend structure follows the principle of keeping route definitions separate from the implementation of business operations.

Routes define the HTTP interface. Middleware handles common checks. Controllers contain resource-specific processing, and models define the structure of persisted records.

\subsection*{Frontend Architecture}

The frontend contains:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item React application components.
    \item Authentication context.
    \item Protected application routes.
    \item Centralized Axios API client.
    \item Reusable resource-management pages.
    \item Dashboard and report views.
    \item Module-specific interfaces for luggage and other operational resources.
\end{itemize}

The frontend therefore has both shared and module-specific components. Shared infrastructure is used for authentication, routing, and API communication, while individual pages provide resource-specific interfaces.

\subsection*{Architectural Advantages}

The selected architecture provides several practical benefits.

First, the frontend and backend can be developed independently as long as the API contract remains stable. Second, security-related checks are centralized in the backend rather than being repeated inside each frontend page. Third, the database structure is encapsulated behind Mongoose models.

The architecture also makes debugging more systematic. A problem can be investigated at the UI, API request, middleware, controller, model, or database level instead of treating the complete application as one indivisible component.


