\section{Database Design}

MongoDB is used as the persistent database, with Mongoose providing schema definitions and database interaction.

The primary collections correspond to the following models:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Users
    \item Flights
    \item Luggage
    \item Staff
    \item Transportation
    \item Visitors
\end{itemize}

The schemas enforce required properties and selected unique constraints.

The database design follows the operational structure of the application. Each major resource has a corresponding model rather than placing unrelated information into one generalized collection.

\subsection*{Flight Model}

The Flight model stores flight number, airline, origin, destination, departure time, arrival time, gate, and flight status.

The flight number is unique, helping prevent multiple records from using the same identifier. The status field uses a defined set of values such as Scheduled, Delayed, Boarding, Departed, Arrived, and Cancelled.

The model also provides the information required by other modules that refer to flights.

\subsection*{Luggage Model}

The Luggage model stores luggage tag, passenger name, flight reference, weight, luggage status, and location.

The luggage tag acts as a unique identifier for an individual luggage record. The flight field is a reference to the corresponding Flight document.

Luggage status is represented using predefined values including checked-in, in-transit, arrived, claimed, and lost.

\subsection*{Staff Model}

The Staff model stores employee ID, name, department, role, contact number, shift, and active/inactive status.

Employee ID is unique. Shift values are restricted to Morning, Evening, and Night, while status distinguishes active and inactive staff records.

\subsection*{Transportation Model}

The Transportation model stores vehicle number, vehicle type, driver name, route, associated flight, scheduled time, and transportation status.

Vehicle type is restricted to Cab or Shuttle in the current implementation. Transportation records can reference Flight documents, allowing the system to associate ground transportation information with a flight.

\subsection*{Visitor Model}

The Visitor model stores visitor name, contact number, purpose, person being visited, pass ID, pass validity, and visitor status.

The pass ID is unique and provides an identifiable value for the visitor record. Validity fields allow the system to store the intended period for which the visitor pass applies.

\subsection*{User Model}

The User model stores name, email, password, role, and timestamps. Passwords are processed using password hashing rather than being stored as plain text.

The email field is unique and normalized to lowercase. The role field uses the defined application roles. Timestamps allow user records to contain creation and update information.

\subsection*{Database Design Principles}

The database design applies several simple but important principles:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item Each major operational resource has its own model.
    \item Required fields are enforced through Mongoose schemas.
    \item Unique identifiers are used where duplicate records would cause ambiguity.
    \item Enumerated fields reduce inconsistent status values.
    \item References are used where an operational entity needs to associate with a Flight.
    \item Database operations are performed through backend controllers rather than directly by the frontend.
\end{enumerate}

These principles provide sufficient structure for the current prototype without introducing unnecessary database complexity.


