% SECURITY DESIGN
% ============================================================

\section{Security Design}

Authentication and authorization are handled by the backend.

The security design follows a layered approach. The frontend provides the user interaction for login, while the backend remains responsible for deciding whether a request is authenticated and authorized.

\subsection*{JWT Authentication}

After successful login, the backend generates a JSON Web Token. The frontend attaches this token to protected API requests using the HTTP Authorization header.

The backend authentication middleware extracts and verifies the token using the configured JWT secret.

JWT is suitable for the current REST API design because the token can accompany requests without requiring the backend to maintain a separate session object for every browser request.

\subsection*{Role-Based Authorization}

After authentication, the role middleware checks whether the user's role belongs to the list of roles permitted for the requested operation.

This creates two levels of protection:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item Authentication -- ``Who is the user?''
    \item Authorization -- ``Is this user allowed to perform this operation?''
\end{enumerate}

The distinction prevents a valid user account from automatically receiving unrestricted access.

\subsection*{Password Protection}

User passwords are processed using \texttt{bcryptjs}. The system therefore does not need to store passwords as plain text.

Password hashing is important because the database should not contain directly usable plaintext credentials. The login operation compares the submitted password against the stored password hash.

\subsection*{Frontend Route Protection}

The frontend also uses protected application routes. This improves user experience by preventing unauthenticated users from navigating directly into the application interface.

However, frontend protection is not treated as the primary security boundary. Backend middleware remains responsible for protecting API operations.

\subsection*{Environment Configuration}

Sensitive configuration values such as the MongoDB connection string and JWT secret are provided through environment variables.

This approach prevents configuration secrets from being hard-coded into source files. Environment configuration also allows development and deployment environments to use different connection values without modifying application logic.

\subsection*{Security Limitations}

The current security design is appropriate for a prototype but does not represent a complete production security framework.

Further production-level improvements could include rate limiting, stronger token lifecycle management, password recovery, audit logging, secure deployment configuration, HTTPS enforcement, and systematic security testing.

These are identified as future improvements rather than claimed features of the current implementation.


