\chapter{Implementation Phase}

\section{Frontend Implementation}

The frontend is implemented using React and Vite.

The application uses React Router for navigation between authenticated modules. Protected routes prevent unauthenticated users from directly accessing application pages.

The frontend follows a component-oriented design. Common functionality such as authentication state and API communication is separated from individual resource pages.

\subsection*{Authentication Context}

The authentication context manages:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Current user information.
    \item Authentication token.
    \item Login operation.
    \item Registration operation.
    \item Logout operation.
    \item Session restoration when the application starts.
\end{itemize}

The frontend stores the authentication token and user information locally and uses the token when communicating with protected backend APIs.

When the application starts with an existing token, the authentication context can request the authenticated user's information from the backend. This allows the application to restore the user session rather than requiring a login on every page refresh.

\subsection*{Protected Routes}

Protected routes provide the navigation-level security layer of the React application.

The route guard checks whether an authenticated user is available before allowing access to the protected application area. If authentication information is unavailable, the user is directed toward the authentication interface.

This provides a clear separation between public authentication pages and the internal application dashboard and modules.

\subsection*{API Client}

Axios is configured as the central HTTP client. The API client uses the configured backend URL and automatically adds the JWT token to authenticated requests.

This avoids duplicating authentication-header logic across individual frontend modules.

The centralized client also provides a consistent location for common HTTP behaviour. For example, an unauthorized API response can be handled centrally instead of requiring every individual page to implement the same logic.

\subsection*{Resource Management}

Reusable frontend components are used for several CRUD-oriented resources. The common resource page pattern provides consistent handling for:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Listing records.
    \item Creating records.
    \item Editing records.
    \item Deleting records.
    \item Displaying API errors.
\end{itemize}

This approach reduces repeated frontend code for resources that follow similar CRUD workflows.

\subsection*{Module-Specific Interface}

Although several modules share common CRUD behaviour, not all resources have identical fields or user interactions. The luggage module, for example, has its own interface requirements because luggage contains status, location, weight, passenger information, and flight association.

The frontend therefore combines reusable structures with module-specific components rather than forcing every resource into exactly the same interface.


\section{Backend Implementation}

The backend is implemented using Node.js and Express.js.

The Express server registers the API routes for the different airport-management modules and connects to MongoDB before accepting application requests.

The principal API groups are:

