\subsection*{Express Server}

The main server initializes the Express application, loads environment configuration, enables required middleware, establishes the database connection, and mounts the API routes.

The server therefore acts as the central integration point for the backend.

The route groups are mounted under the common \texttt{/api} prefix, while individual route files define the operations for their respective resources.

\subsection*{Controllers}

Controllers contain the implementation of resource-level operations.

The controllers receive requests from routes, process the relevant information, interact with the appropriate model, and return an HTTP response.

This organization prevents the route definitions from becoming overloaded with database logic.

\subsection*{Middleware}

The backend uses authentication and role middleware.

Authentication middleware verifies the JWT and makes the authenticated user information available to subsequent processing.

Role middleware receives the permitted roles for an operation and compares them with the authenticated user's role.

An important design characteristic is that role middleware does not need to query MongoDB for every authorization decision. The authenticated user information is obtained from the verified JWT and is then used by the role-checking layer.

\subsection*{Models}

Mongoose models represent the persistent entities.

Each model defines the expected fields, data types, required properties, constraints, references, and enumerated values where applicable.

This provides a consistent data structure between the backend controller logic and MongoDB.

\subsection*{Error Handling}

The backend returns appropriate HTTP responses for common failure conditions such as missing authentication, invalid authorization, invalid data, unavailable records, and database-related failures.

Returning meaningful status codes is important because the frontend can use these responses to determine whether an operation succeeded or needs to be reported to the user.


\section{Authentication Implementation}

The authentication module provides registration and login endpoints.

During registration:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item User information is received by the backend.
    \item Password information is processed securely.
    \item A User document is created in MongoDB.
\end{enumerate}

During login:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item The email is located in the database.
    \item The submitted password is compared with the stored password hash.
    \item A JWT is generated after successful validation.
    \item The token and user information are returned to the frontend.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Authentication Request Lifecycle}

The authentication lifecycle can be summarized as:

\[
\text{Credentials}
\rightarrow
\text{Authentication Controller}
\rightarrow
\text{User Lookup}
\rightarrow
\text{Password Verification}
\rightarrow
\text{JWT Generation}
\rightarrow
\text{Frontend Session}
\]

For subsequent protected requests, the token is sent back to the server in the Authorization header.

The backend then verifies the token before allowing access to the protected controller.

\subsection*{Current User Endpoint}

The authenticated-user endpoint allows the frontend to retrieve information associated with the currently authenticated token.

This endpoint is useful during session restoration because the frontend can validate an existing token and obtain the corresponding user information.


\section{Flight Management Implementation}

The Flight module supports:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Creating flights.
    \item Retrieving flight records.
    \item Retrieving an individual flight.
    \item Updating flight information.
    \item Deleting flights where permitted.
\end{itemize}

Flight records contain a unique flight number and operational information including airline, origin, destination, gate, timing, and status.

The module provides one of the central datasets for the system because both luggage and transportation records can contain references to flights.

The controller also handles retrieval and sorting of flight information so that the frontend can present operational records in an appropriate order.

\subsection*{Flight Status}

The current Flight model supports the following status values:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Scheduled
    \item Delayed
    \item Boarding
    \item Departed
    \item Arrived
    \item Cancelled
\end{itemize}

Using a defined status set reduces inconsistent representations of flight state.


\section{Luggage Management Implementation}

The Luggage module maintains luggage information associated with flights.

Each luggage record contains a unique luggage tag and can store passenger name, flight reference, weight, status, and location.

The supported luggage statuses include:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Checked-in
    \item In-transit
    \item Arrived
    \item Claimed
    \item Lost
\end{itemize}

The luggage module demonstrates how a dependent operational record can reference another database entity. Instead of storing a complete copy of the flight information, the luggage record contains a Flight reference.

This provides a meaningful relationship between the two resources while keeping their individual models separate.

\subsection*{Luggage Tracking Information}

The combination of luggage tag, status, and location provides a basic tracking representation.

The current implementation should be understood as a database-based management module rather than a physical real-time tracking system. It records the information supplied through the application but does not claim to receive sensor or airport-baggage-system data.


\section{Visitor Management Implementation}

The Visitor module maintains airport visitor records.

Each record can contain visitor name, contact information, purpose, person to visit, pass ID, pass validity period, and visitor status.

The supported visitor states include Registered, Active, Expired, and Cancelled.

The visitor module provides structured management of visitor information and ensures that records can be identified through a unique pass ID.

The validity fields also allow the application to represent the intended active period of a visitor pass.


\section{Staff Management Implementation}

The Staff module stores employee information including employee ID, name, department, role, contact number, shift, and employment status.

The system supports staff CRUD operations subject to role-based permissions.

Staff records are separate from User records. A Staff record represents an airport employee's operational information, whereas a User record represents an account used to authenticate with the application.

This distinction avoids assuming that every operational staff record must automatically represent an application account.


\section{Transportation Management Implementation}

The Transportation module manages airport ground transportation records.

Each transportation record contains:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Vehicle number
    \item Vehicle type
    \item Driver name
    \item Route
    \item Associated flight
    \item Scheduled time
    \item Transportation status
\end{itemize}

Transportation records may be associated with Flight documents through MongoDB references.

The current vehicle types include Cab and Shuttle. The status field represents states such as Scheduled, In Transit, Completed, and Cancelled.

The module demonstrates the same architecture as the other resources: frontend form and list interface, Axios request, Express route, authorization middleware, controller, Mongoose model, and MongoDB persistence.


\section{Dashboard and Reports Implementation}

The dashboard provides a centralized interface for airport information.

The frontend requests information from multiple backend APIs and uses the returned data to populate operational views.

The reports section provides a higher-level presentation of available operational information.

The current implementation is primarily a management and presentation interface rather than a dedicated advanced analytics engine. Therefore, report values should be interpreted according to the data currently available through the implemented modules.

\subsection*{Dashboard Design Purpose}

The dashboard acts as an entry point into the application after authentication. Its purpose is to provide users with a quick overview before they enter a specific operational module.

Because information is obtained from multiple resources, the dashboard demonstrates the integration of the separate APIs at the frontend level.

The dashboard should therefore be viewed as an aggregation and presentation feature rather than a separate database entity.

\subsection*{Reports Design Purpose}

The reports interface provides a more summarized view of available operational information.

It is important to distinguish this interface from an advanced analytics system. The current implementation does not claim to provide predictive airport analytics or complex statistical models. Its purpose is to present information available from the implemented application modules.

\subsection*{Settings}

The settings interface provides application-level information and configuration-oriented presentation.

Settings is treated as a frontend application feature and not as a database-backed operational entity. This distinction is reflected in the class diagram.


