\chapter{Results and Evaluation}

\section{Testing Methodology}

The current testing approach focuses on verifying the major application workflows and API functionality.

Testing is divided into the following levels:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{API testing:} Backend endpoints are tested for correct request handling and response behaviour.
    \item \textbf{Authentication testing:} Registration, login, token validation, and unauthorized access are checked.
    \item \textbf{Authorization testing:} Requests from different user roles are checked against role restrictions.
    \item \textbf{CRUD testing:} Create, read, update, and delete operations are tested for the core resources.
    \item \textbf{Frontend integration testing:} Frontend pages are checked for correct communication with backend APIs.
    \item \textbf{System testing:} Complete user workflows are checked from login through access to management modules.
\end{enumerate}

The testing approach is primarily functional and integration-oriented. It verifies whether the components behave correctly together rather than attempting to claim a complete automated test framework.

\subsection*{API Testing Approach}

API testing focuses on the route-level behaviour of the backend.

For protected endpoints, testing includes requests with no token, invalid tokens, valid tokens with insufficient roles, and valid authorized requests.

For CRUD modules, the basic lifecycle is verified:

\[
\text{Create}
\rightarrow
\text{Read}
\rightarrow
\text{Update}
\rightarrow
\text{Read Again}
\rightarrow
\text{Delete}
\]

This sequence is useful because it verifies not only individual operations but also whether changes persist across requests.

\subsection*{Authentication Testing Approach}

Authentication testing checks both successful and unsuccessful paths.

Successful registration should create a valid user record. Successful login should return a JWT. Invalid credentials should be rejected.

Protected resources are then tested using the returned token.

\subsection*{Authorization Testing Approach}

Authorization testing is particularly important because SAMS supports multiple roles.

A valid JWT does not automatically mean that every operation should succeed. The role middleware checks the authenticated user's role against the roles permitted by the route.

Testing therefore considers both authentication and authorization as separate conditions.


