\section{Results}

The current prototype demonstrates the following implemented capabilities:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item User registration and login are implemented.
    \item JWT-based authentication is implemented.
    \item Role-based authorization is implemented.
    \item Flight CRUD functionality is implemented.
    \item Luggage CRUD functionality is implemented.
    \item Visitor CRUD functionality is implemented.
    \item Staff CRUD functionality is implemented.
    \item Transportation CRUD functionality is implemented.
    \item React frontend pages are connected to backend APIs.
    \item MongoDB is used for persistent storage.
    \item Dashboard and reports interfaces are available.
\end{itemize}

The prototype therefore demonstrates a functioning full-stack airport-management architecture rather than only a static frontend.

\subsection*{Authentication Result}

The authentication architecture successfully establishes a session between the frontend and backend through JWT.

The separation of login from protected resource requests demonstrates that the application does not require every operational API to independently implement credential verification.

\subsection*{CRUD Result}

The major operational modules follow a consistent CRUD pattern. This demonstrates that the backend can persist, retrieve, modify, and remove records according to the implemented authorization rules.

The use of individual models also means that the database structure reflects the different categories of airport information rather than storing unrelated information in one generic record.

\subsection*{Integration Result}

The most significant result of the project is the integration of multiple layers.

A working management page requires successful interaction between the React component, Axios API client, Express route, middleware, controller, Mongoose model, and MongoDB database.

Therefore, the project demonstrates full-stack integration rather than isolated implementation of frontend and backend components.

\subsection*{Observed Limitations}

The following limitations remain in the current prototype:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Automated unit and integration tests have not yet been added.
    \item Advanced airport analytics are limited compared with a production airport-management platform.
    \item Dashboard/report presentation contains some demonstration-oriented values and should be further aligned with fully calculated database-backed metrics.
    \item Additional validation and edge-case handling can be expanded.
    \item Production deployment, monitoring, logging, and automated CI/CD are outside the current prototype scope.
\end{itemize}

The limitations are important because they define the boundary of what can reasonably be claimed for the current project.

The prototype demonstrates the architecture and core operational workflows, but it should not be interpreted as a complete production airport-management solution.

\subsection*{Security and Privacy Analysis}

The backend architecture places authentication and authorization at the API level rather than relying solely on frontend restrictions.

JWT middleware prevents unauthenticated users from accessing protected endpoints. Role middleware provides an additional authorization layer.

Passwords are processed using \texttt{bcryptjs}. Configuration values such as the MongoDB URI and JWT secret are supplied through environment variables rather than being embedded directly into source code.

For a production deployment, HTTPS, stronger secret management, token lifecycle improvements, rate limiting, audit logging, and additional security testing should be introduced.

\subsection*{Data Integrity Analysis}

Data integrity is supported through Mongoose schemas.

Required fields prevent important records from being created without necessary information. Unique constraints reduce duplicate identifiers. Enumerated values limit status fields to predefined alternatives.

References between related models provide an additional structural relationship between operational entities.

These measures do not eliminate every possible data-quality problem, but they provide a meaningful baseline for the prototype.

\subsection*{Usability Analysis}

The frontend uses common layouts and reusable resource-management structures for several modules.

A consistent CRUD interaction pattern means that users can encounter similar controls when moving between different management areas. This reduces the amount of new interaction behaviour a user needs to learn.

The dashboard provides a central starting point after authentication, while individual module pages provide detailed management interfaces.


\section{Analysis}

The implementation demonstrates that the selected architecture is suitable for the current prototype.

\subsection*{Functional Analysis}

The most important functional component is the combination of authentication, authorization, and resource management. Each major airport entity is represented through its own Mongoose model, controller, and route group.

This modular organization allows the application to support several operational domains without combining all business logic into one large controller.

\subsection*{Module Interaction Analysis}

Although the modules are separate, they are not completely independent.

Flights provide information that can be referenced by luggage and transportation records. Authentication provides the security context used by all protected modules. Dashboard and reporting interfaces consume information from multiple modules.

This creates a relationship between modularity and integration. Modules remain separately implemented while still participating in common application workflows.

\subsection*{Trade-off Analysis}

Several implementation decisions were relevant to the system design:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{MongoDB vs. relational database:} MongoDB provides a flexible document-oriented model that is suitable for the current prototype and works naturally with Mongoose.
    \item \textbf{JWT vs. server-side sessions:} JWT provides a straightforward mechanism for stateless API authentication and is convenient for a React frontend communicating with REST endpoints.
    \item \textbf{Centralized API client vs. direct requests:} A shared Axios client reduces duplication and provides a single location for authentication headers and common response handling.
    \item \textbf{Reusable frontend components vs. separate pages:} Reusable resource components reduce duplication across CRUD-oriented management modules.
    \item \textbf{Role middleware vs. frontend-only restrictions:} Backend role middleware ensures that access restrictions cannot be bypassed simply by manipulating the frontend.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Maintainability}

The separation between routes, controllers, middleware, and models makes the backend easier to understand and extend.

For example, a future airport module can follow the existing pattern:

\[
\text{Model}
\rightarrow
\text{Controller}
\rightarrow
\text{Route}
\rightarrow
\text{Frontend API}
\rightarrow
\text{React Page}
\]

This provides a repeatable structure for future development.

\subsection*{Debugging and Maintenance Perspective}

The modular design also provides a useful debugging strategy.

If a frontend page does not display expected data, the issue can be investigated progressively:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item Check whether the React component sends the request.
    \item Check the Axios request URL.
    \item Check whether the JWT is attached.
    \item Check whether the Express route receives the request.
    \item Check authentication and role middleware.
    \item Check controller processing.
    \item Check Mongoose model interaction.
    \item Check the database result.
\end{enumerate}

This layered debugging process is one of the practical advantages of the selected architecture.

\subsection*{Prototype vs. Production Analysis}

The current system should be classified as a functional prototype.

A production airport-management system would normally require considerably more integration, monitoring, reliability engineering, security controls, auditing, testing, and deployment infrastructure.

The present project instead focuses on demonstrating the software engineering principles required to build the core application architecture.

This distinction is important for evaluating the project fairly. The goal of the current milestone is not to claim that every airport operational process has been automated, but to demonstrate a coherent full-stack system that can support structured airport-management workflows.


