\chapter{Conclusion}

\section{Summary of Work}

Smart Airport Management System (SAMS) is a full-stack web application developed to centralize important airport operational management activities.

The frontend is implemented using React and communicates with an Express.js REST API through Axios. The backend provides authentication, role-based authorization, and CRUD APIs for flights, luggage, visitors, staff, and transportation. MongoDB provides persistent storage through Mongoose models.

Authentication is implemented using JWT, while passwords are protected using bcryptjs. Role-based middleware ensures that different users receive access according to their responsibilities.

The project also includes a centralized dashboard, reports interface, settings, protected routes, and reusable frontend resource-management components.

The current prototype therefore establishes the core architecture of a Smart Airport Management System and provides a foundation for future expansion.

The development process also demonstrates the relationship between software requirements and implementation. Functional requirements were translated into backend APIs and frontend interfaces, while non-functional requirements such as security, maintainability, and data integrity influenced the architecture.

\section{Key Findings}

The project leads to the following findings:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item \textbf{Centralized management improves organization.} Keeping flights, luggage, visitors, staff, and transportation within one system provides a consistent operational interface.
   
    \item \textbf{Role-based access is essential.} Different airport personnel have different responsibilities, making backend authorization an important security requirement.
   
    \item \textbf{REST APIs provide clear separation.} Separating the React frontend from the Express backend allows both layers to evolve independently.
   
    \item \textbf{Schema validation improves data integrity.} Mongoose schemas provide required fields, enumerations, and uniqueness constraints for operational records.
   
    \item \textbf{Modular architecture improves maintainability.} Separating models, controllers, routes, and middleware makes individual modules easier to maintain.
   
    \item \textbf{The current prototype provides a foundation rather than a complete production airport platform.} Additional monitoring, analytics, testing, deployment, and integration capabilities would be required for production-scale use.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Learning Outcomes}

The project provides practical experience in several areas of software engineering.

The development of the frontend demonstrates component-based user-interface design and client-side routing. Backend development demonstrates REST API design, controller organization, middleware, and database integration. Authentication demonstrates the practical use of JWT and password hashing.

The database design demonstrates how application requirements can be translated into structured Mongoose models and relationships.

The project also demonstrates the importance of integration testing. A complete feature depends on several layers functioning together, so testing only one component is not sufficient to establish that the complete workflow works correctly.

\section{Future Work and Recommendations}

The following improvements are recommended for subsequent development:

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item Add automated unit and integration testing.
    \item Introduce automated API test suites for all CRUD endpoints.
    \item Add more advanced dashboard analytics based entirely on database-derived metrics.
    \item Add audit logs for sensitive administrative operations.
    \item Add stronger authentication features such as refresh-token rotation and password recovery.
    \item Add pagination, filtering, and advanced search for large operational datasets.
    \item Add real-time flight-status updates.
    \item Introduce notification mechanisms for important operational events.
    \item Improve transportation tracking and vehicle availability management.
    \item Add more detailed passenger and airport-staff workflows.
    \item Introduce production deployment, monitoring, centralized logging, and CI/CD.
    \item Strengthen security through rate limiting, secure cookie/token strategies, HTTPS enforcement, and systematic security testing.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Potential Development Direction}

Future development should preserve the current modular architecture rather than replacing it unnecessarily.

For example, a new module can be added by following the established pattern of database model, controller, route, frontend API operation, and React interface.

This approach would allow the system to grow gradually while maintaining a consistent architecture.

\subsection*{Overall Findings}

SAMS successfully demonstrates the core idea of a centralized, role-aware airport-management platform. Its current architecture is sufficiently modular to support future expansion without requiring a complete redesign.

The most important achievement of the current milestone is the integration of the frontend, backend, authentication layer, role authorization, REST APIs, and MongoDB persistence into a single working prototype.

The project also demonstrates an important software engineering principle: a useful management system is not simply a collection of screens. Its functionality depends on the interaction between requirements, user roles, APIs, business operations, database structures, validation, and testing.

The current prototype provides this complete foundation and can therefore serve as a base for further development toward a more comprehensive airport-management platform.


