\subsection*{Authentication Endpoint Behaviour}

The registration endpoint is responsible for creating a new User record. The login endpoint validates credentials and returns authentication information. The \texttt{/me} endpoint is protected and is used to retrieve information associated with the authenticated token.

\subsection*{Operational Endpoint Behaviour}

The operational modules follow the REST CRUD pattern.

The GET operation retrieves records, POST creates records, PUT modifies existing records, and DELETE removes records where the user's role is permitted to perform the operation.

The exact authorization rules are defined in the backend route middleware and therefore remain part of the implementation rather than being inferred solely from the frontend interface.


\chapter{Project Structure}

The implemented project follows a separation between frontend and backend components.

\begin{verbatim}
SAMS/
|
+-- backend/
|   +-- src/
|       +-- config/
|       +-- controllers/
|       +-- middleware/
|       +-- models/
|       +-- routes/
|       +-- server.js
|
+-- frontend/
    +-- src/
        +-- components/
        +-- context/
        +-- lib/
        +-- pages/
        +-- App.jsx
        +-- main.jsx
\end{verbatim}

The exact directory names may vary according to the final merged repository structure.

\subsection*{Backend Directory Responsibilities}

The \texttt{config} directory contains database configuration. The \texttt{controllers} directory contains request-processing logic. The \texttt{middleware} directory contains authentication and authorization functions.

The \texttt{models} directory contains Mongoose schemas and models, while the \texttt{routes} directory defines the API interface.

The main server file initializes the application and connects the individual route groups.

\subsection*{Frontend Directory Responsibilities}

The frontend source is organized around React application components, context, API utilities, pages, and application routing.

The authentication context provides shared authentication state. The API utility provides centralized HTTP communication, while pages provide the visible resource-management interfaces.

This division supports reuse and keeps application-wide functionality separate from individual screens.


\chapter{Deployment and Execution}

\section*{Backend}

The backend requires the required environment variables to be configured, including the MongoDB connection string, port, and JWT secret.

The backend can be started using:

\begin{verbatim}
npm install
npm run dev
\end{verbatim}

For the production start command:

\begin{verbatim}
npm start
\end{verbatim}

Before starting the server, the MongoDB connection must be available and the required environment variables must be configured.

\section*{Frontend}

The frontend dependencies can be installed using:

\begin{verbatim}
npm install
\end{verbatim}

The development server can then be started using:

\begin{verbatim}
npm run dev
\end{verbatim}

The frontend communicates with the backend through the configured API URL.

\subsection*{Execution Sequence}

A typical local development sequence is:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
    \item Configure the backend environment variables.
    \item Ensure that the MongoDB database is accessible.
    \item Install backend dependencies.
    \item Start the backend development server.
    \item Install frontend dependencies.
    \item Start the Vite development server.
    \item Open the frontend in a web browser.
    \item Register or log in to the application.
    \item Access the permitted operational modules.
\end{enumerate}

The frontend and backend therefore operate as separate development processes while communicating through the REST API.

\subsection*{Configuration Considerations}

The API URL used by the frontend should correspond to the running backend server.

The backend configuration should contain the MongoDB connection information and JWT secret required by the application. These values should be supplied through environment configuration rather than committed directly to the repository.

\subsection*{Prototype Deployment Scope}

The current deployment description is intended for local or development execution of the prototype.

A production deployment would additionally require a suitable hosting environment, HTTPS configuration, secure environment-variable management, database deployment configuration, monitoring, logging, and an appropriate build/deployment pipeline.


